they're fighting  on several fronts in india fighting to  save lives  but also fighting to tell it as it is  and those at this hospital in lucknow  are now facing police charges for doing  just that  every covered patient who arrives here  desperately needs oxygen  but those who sounded the alarm about  oxygen shortages at this hospital  are now being charged under the national  security act  and face being arrested at any moment  [Music]  the head of this small hospital says  four of his patients died in one day  when oxygen ran out  he showed me the messages he put out  desperately asking for help  when he got no response he put out a  notice telling relatives they should  take the patients elsewhere  a number of them did but others had no  choice  the hospital head took us to see his  small oxygen store  it's again at dangerously low levels the  state's high court has said  not supplying oxygen to hospitals is a  criminal act  no less than genocide  but the oxygen ran out by nine o'clock  that morning  the relatives of those who died say  that's what killed  them  if the oxygen had been there my aunt and  cousin would not have died he told us  as we traveled through uttar pradesh  with its huge population  of 200 million we found many ignoring  covert warnings  this is meant to be locked down still  bustling with people  and few masks  in the shadow of india's most famous  monument they appeared  oblivious to the virus  doctors we've spoken to say the mutation  which has spread to other countries  including britain  is a far more aggressive infectious  variant  and affecting far younger people it  seems that the virus is more lethal this  time  it seems so because the number of deaths  have been a little higher than what was  what we saw last time also what we are  observing is  last year it took some time for the  patient to deteriorate  but with this virus the infectivity or  the  aggressiveness of the virus is so high  that it hardly takes a day  for the patient otherwise fine to  desaturate and  get compromised  there's a fear about contradicting the  official numbers  but this crematorium in agra has run out  of wood  and is now using cow dung for its  funeral pies  nearly two-thirds of indians live in  remote  rural areas unable to get any help for  covid  with many dying at home and untested  the workers have no protective outfits  against the virus  whilst telling us they've never been so  busy but they're reluctant to give exact  figures  why don't you just tell them the young  boy behind him says  he gets a furious response  in these areas of such deprivation where  religious sadhus are often the only  people recognising the dead  we may never know the real toll of the  pandemic in india  alex crawford sky news in uttar pradesh

